{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a0da2c37f58cb832", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tAgreement-Based Audio-Visual Segmentation: Champion Report for the MeViS-Audio Track in the 8th LSVOS Challenge\n2\t\n3\tReport GitHub Issue\n4\t\n5\t ×\n6\t\n7\t Title:\n8\t\n9\tContent selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n10\t\n11\t Description:\n12\t\n13\t Submit without GitHub\n14\t Submit in GitHub\n15\t\n16\t arXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n17\t Learn more\n18\t ×\n19\t\n20\t Back to arXiv\n21\t\n22\t Why HTML?\n23\t\n24\t Report Issue\n25\t\n26\t Back to Abstract\n27\t\n28\t Download PDF\n29\t\n30\tAbstract\n31\t\n32\t1 Introduction\n33\t\n34\t2 Challenge and Dataset\n35\t\n36\t3 Method\n37\t\n38\t3.1 Overview\n39\t\n40\t3.2 Audio Transcription\n41\t\n42\t3.3 Candidate Mask Tracks\n43\t\n44\tPoint-guided SAM3 track.\n45\t\n46\tComplementary tracks.\n47\t\n48\t3.4 Agreement-Based Track Selection\n49\t\n50\t3.5 Structured Correction\n51\t\n52\t3.6 Target-Presence Classification\n53\t\n54\t4 Experiments\n55\t\n56\t4.1 Evaluation Protocol\n57\t\n58\t4.2 Implementation Details\n59\t\n60\t4.3 Mask Components\n61\t\n62\t4.4 Presence Classification\n63\t\n64\t4.5 Official Test Result\n65\t\n66\t5 Discussion and Limitations\n67\t\n68\t6 Conclusion\n69\t\n70\tReferences\n71\t\n72\t License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n73\t\n74\tarXiv:2608.09475v1 [cs.CV] 10 Aug 2026\n75\t\n76\t11institutetext: National 111 Project Base of Intelligent Information Processing\n77\t\n78\tAgreement-Based Audio-Visual Segmentation: Champion Report for the MeViS-Audio Track in the 8th LSVOS Challenge\n79\t\n80\tYiwen Ren\n81\t\n82\tJianing Liu\n83\t\n84\tYingxin Wang\n85\t\n86\tKexin Zhang\n87\t\n88\tLicheng Jiao\n89\t\n90\tLingling Li\n91\t\n92\tXu Liu\n93\t\n94\tAbstract\n95\t\n96\tThe MeViS-Audio track asks a system to segment the objects described by a\n97\tspoken motion expression throughout a video and to return empty masks when the\n98\tdescribed target is absent. We present a simple staged solution. Qwen3-ASR\n99\tfirst converts speech into text. Several video mask tracks are then produced\n100\twith complementary grounding and segmentation models. Instead of trusting a\n101\tsingle prediction, we select the track that has the highest average mask\n102\tagreement with the other candidates. A small set of explicit direction,\n103\tcount, and plural rules corrects queries that require more than ordinary\n104\tsingle-object tracking. Finally, a video-level classifier combines visual,\n105\taudio-visual, and within-video query scores to decide whether any target is\n106\tpresent. The submitted system obtains 0.5952 𝒥&ℱ\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}, 0.7931 no-target\n107\taccuracy, 0.9205 target accuracy, and a final score of 0.769589. The challenge\n108\torganizers notified our team that this result ranked first in the track.\n109\t\n110\t1 Introduction\n111\t\n112\tReferring video object segmentation predicts a binary mask sequence for the\n113\tobject described by a language query. MeViS emphasizes motion expressions,\n114\twhich often cannot be resolved from a single frame [4, 5]. The Audio track adds another source of uncertainty: the query\n115\tis spoken and must first be understood from audio. Recognition errors can\n116\tchange the object, its motion, or its number. Moreover, some queries have no\n117\tmatching target in the video, so a segmenter must sometimes return an empty\n118\tsequence rather than the most plausible visible object.\n119\t\n120\tEarlier MeViS-Audio systems established a useful modular design. APRVOS uses\n121\tspeech recognition, visual existence checking, video segmentation, and mask\n122\trefinement [8]. ASR-SaSaSa2VA combines Qwen3-ASR,\n123\tSaSaSa2VA, and a separate no-target classifier [13]. VIRST-Audio\n124\talso separates transcription, segmentation, and existence prediction\n125\t [7]. These systems show that speech recognition,\n126\tpixel-level tracking, and target existence are different decisions and should\n127\tnot be forced into one output score.\n128\t\n129\tOur system follows this modular view but reduces dependence on any single\n130\tvideo segmenter. It creates several full-video mask tracks and selects the\n131\tone most consistent with the others. The selection uses only predicted masks,\n132\tnot validation or test labels. Explicit motion and count rules are applied\n133\tonly to queries for which the ordinary single-target assumption is\n134\tinsufficient. A final presence classifier handles no-target inputs while\n135\tprotecting high-confidence structured predictions.\n136\t\n137\tThe main contributions are:\n138\t\n139\t• \n140\t\n141\tan audio-to-mask pipeline that combines strong open models without\n142\tend-to-end retraining;\n143\t\n144\t• \n145\t\n146\ta label-free, agreement-based rule for selecting a complete video mask\n147\ttrack, followed by a small structured correction stage\n148\t\n149\t• \n150\t\n151\ta video-level target-presence classifier designed to maintain high\n152\trecall on valid targets while improving no-target accuracy.\n153\t\n154\t2 Challenge and Dataset\n155\t\n156\tThe 8th LSVOS Challenge at ECCV 2026 contains three tracks. The\n157\tMOSEv2 track evaluates class-agnostic video object segmentation in\n158\tcrowded scenes with occlusion, disappearance, small objects, and distractors;\n159\tit uses the MOSEv2 dataset [6]. The\n160\tMeViS-Text track evaluates referring video object segmentation from\n161\twritten motion expressions. The MeViS-Audio track uses spoken\n162\tversions of motion expressions and additionally evaluates whether the referred\n163\ttarget exists. Both referring tracks are based on MeViS v2\n164\t [5].\n165\t\n166\tMeViS contains 2,006 videos, 8,171 annotated objects, and 33,072 motion\n167\texpressions in text and audio [5]. Unlike referring\n168\texpressions dominated by color or object category, many MeViS queries describe\n169\trelative motion, temporal order, or interactions. Correctly identifying the\n170\ttarget therefore requires evidence across time. For the Audio track, the\n171\tinput is a video V={It}t=1TV=\\{I_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} and an audio query AA. The output is a\n172\tmask sequence M={mt}t=1TM=\\{m_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T}. If the query has no matching target, all\n173\tmtm_{t} must be empty.\n174\t\n175\t3 Method\n176\t\n177\t3.1 Overview\n178\t\n179\tFigure 1 shows the complete inference path. The method first\n180\ttranscribes the audio, then builds several candidate mask tracks. An\n181\tagreement rule chooses one complete track per query. Structured corrections\n182\tare applied to a small set of direction, count, and plural queries. Finally,\n183\tthe presence classifier either keeps the masks or replaces the whole sequence\n184\twith empty masks.\n185\t\n186\tFigure 1: System overview. Audio is transcribed once, and all visual modules\n187\treceive the same text query. Candidate tracks are selected by mask agreement;\n188\tthe final presence decision either keeps the masks or returns an empty sequence.\n189\t\n190\t3.2 Audio Transcription\n191\t\n192\tWe use Qwen3-ASR-1.7B [12] to convert the audio query into a\n193\ttext expression\n194\t\n195\tq=ASR⁡(A).q=\\operatorname{ASR}(A).\n196\t\n197\t(1)\n198\t\n199\tKeeping transcription separate makes the visual stages easier to inspect and\n200\tallows all candidate generators to receive exactly the same expression. The\n201\tASR model produced transcripts for all 444 test queries.\n202\t\n203\t3.3 Candidate Mask Tracks\n204\t\n205\tPoint-guided SAM3 track.\n206\t\n207\tThe main candidate uses MolmoPoint-8B [3] to predict\n208\tobject points with timestamps from the video and transcript. The video is\n209\tencoded at 6 frames per second and the prompt requests points at 1 frame per\n210\tsecond. We use at most 128 input frames, with deterministic 96- and 64-frame\n211\tfallbacks when necessary. These points initialize SAM3\n212\t [2], which propagates masks through the original video. A\n213\tparallel SAM2.1 propagation [11] provides an independent\n214\tconsistency signal for the frame-level fallback path.\n215\t\n216\tComplementary tracks.\n217\t\n218\tWe retain three candidates with different error patterns. SaSaSa2VA-26B\n219\t [9] directly predicts a text-conditioned video mask track\n220\tusing its wrap-around-plus inference mode. A frame-level planner chooses\n221\tbetween point-guided and fallback masks using point hits, agreement between\n222\tSAM2.1 and SAM3, support from neighboring frames, and mask similarity. A\n223\tstructured track is generated for queries that explicitly state horizontal\n224\tdirection, object count, or plural subjects. Qwen3-VL-30B-A3B\n225\t [1] reads 16 sampled frames to extract these constraints,\n226\tand SAM3 forms the corresponding candidate tracks.\n227\t\n228\tThe point-guided route is available for 407 of the 444 test queries. Queries\n229\twithout a complete set of candidates use the full-coverage frame-level track.\n230\t\n231\t3.4 Agreement-Based Track Selection\n232\t\n233\tFor a query with KK candidate tracks, let MitM_{i}^{t} be the mask from candidate\n234\tii at frame tt. We first compute the mean temporal overlap between every\n235\tpair:\n236\t\n237\ta​(i,j)=1T​∑t=1TIoU⁡(Mit,Mjt).a(i,j)=\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\operatorname{IoU}(M_{i}^{t},M_{j}^{t}).\n238\t\n239\t(2)\n240\t\n241\tWhen both masks are empty, their frame-level overlap is defined as one. The\n242\tscore for candidate ii is its mean agreement with all other tracks,\n243\t\n244\ts​(i)=1K−1​∑j≠ia​(i,j),i∗=arg⁡maxi⁡s​(i).s(i)=\\frac{1}{K-1}\\sum_{j\\neq i}a(i,j),\\qquad i^{*}=\\arg\\max_{i}s(i).\n245\t\n246\t(3)\n247\t\n248\tThe output is the complete track Mi∗M_{i^{*}}. This is the medoid of the candidate\n249\tset under mask overlap, but in the remainder of the paper we simply call it\n250\tagreement-based selection. It needs no ground-truth labels and avoids\n251\tcalibrating confidence values from unrelated models.\n252\t\n253\tOn the test set, the selected source was point-guided SAM3 for 403 queries and\n254\tthe structured candidate for two. The frame-level and SaSaSa2VA candidates\n255\twere each selected once. These counts cover the 407 queries with a full\n256\tcandidate set.\n257\t\n258\t3.5 Structured Correction\n259\t\n260\tOrdinary track selection can preserve a visually plausible answer while still\n261\tmissing a constraint such as “moving left,” “two people,” or a plural\n262\tsubject. We therefore apply a separate, conservative correction after\n263\tagreement selection. A query is considered only if its transcript contains\n264\tan explicit direction, number, or plural construction. Candidate tracks are\n265\tthen checked for the requested horizontal motion and count; plural targets are\n266\tcombined by mask union. This stage changed 25 of the 444 test queries. The\n267\trules and thresholds were fixed before test inference.\n268\t\n269\t3.6 Target-Presence Classification\n270\t\n271\tThe final stage predicts whether the transcript has a matching visual target.\n272\tIt combines three scores: a Qwen3-VL-8B visual score, a direct Qwen2.5-Omni\n273\taudio-visual score [14], and the relative rank of the query\n274\tamong all expressions for the same video. For each score we compute seven\n275\tfeatures: its raw value, within-video percentile, standard and robust\n276\tzz-scores, gaps to the video-level maximum and minimum, and the logarithm of\n277\tthe number of queries. The resulting 21 features are passed to a balanced\n278\tlogistic regression classifier.\n279\t\n280\tThe feature design and regularization were fixed on the training data. For\n281\tthe development domain, the classifier weights and threshold were refit with\n282\tvideo-grouped five-fold predictions. The threshold was selected under a\n283\tminimum target recall of 0.97. If the predicted probability is below the\n284\tthreshold, all masks are replaced by empty masks. As a safeguard, a query\n285\taccepted by the structured correction is not removed solely by the presence\n286\tclassifier. This safeguard restored three structured test predictions. The\n287\tfinal classifier explicitly marked 42 test queries as no-target.\n288\t\n289\t4 Experiments\n290\t\n291\t4.1 Evaluation Protocol\n292\t\n293\tMask quality is measured with region overlap 𝒥\\mathcal{J} and boundary accuracy ℱ\\mathcal{F}\n294\tfollowing DAVIS [10]; 𝒥&ℱ\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F} is their mean. Target\n295\taccuracy (T-acc.) is the fraction of target-present queries kept by the\n296\tpresence classifier, while no-target accuracy (N-acc.) is the fraction of\n297\ttarget-absent queries correctly rejected. The challenge score is\n298\t\n299\tFinal=𝒥&ℱ+N-acc.+T-acc.3.\\text{Final}=\\frac{\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}+\\text{N-acc.}+\\text{T-acc.}}{3}.\n300\t\n301\t(4)\n302\t\n303\tMask component studies use a frozen set of 100 target-present queries from the\n304\tofficial valid_u split. Presence results are reported as out-of-fold\n305\t(OOF) predictions with videos kept intact across folds. No test masks or\n306\tper-query test labels were available when fixing the final rules.\n307\t\n308\t4.2 Implementation Details\n309\t\n310\tQwen3-ASR-1.7B is used without task-specific fine-tuning. MolmoPoint-8B runs\n311\tin 4-bit form, and Qwen3-VL-30B-A3B uses its released AWQ weights. SAM3 and\n312\tSAM2.1 propagate masks at the original frame resolution. The SaSaSa2VA\n313\tcandidate is the 26B checkpoint. Agreement selection uses the same four\n314\tcandidate types and the same\n315\trule on validation and test data. The final archive was checked against the\n316\tsample submission and contains 28,415 PNG masks in 50 video directories.\n317\t\n318\t4.3 Mask Components\n319\t\n320\tTable 1 isolates the two decisions added after the main\n321\tpoint-guided SAM3 candidate. Agreement-based selection improves both region\n322\tand boundary quality. Structured correction brings a further 1.15-point\n323\tgain in 𝒥&ℱ\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}, with the largest change in queries involving direction or\n324\tmultiple objects.\n325\t\n326\tTable 1: Mask component study on 100 target-present valid_u queries.\n327\tAll values are percentages.\n328\t\n329\tMethod\n330\t\n331\t𝒥\\mathcal{J}\n332\t\n333\tℱ\\mathcal{F}\n334\t\n335\t𝒥&ℱ\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}\n336\t\n337\tPoint-guided SAM3\n338\t\n339\t71.07\n340\t\n341\t78.13\n342\t\n343\t74.60\n344\t\n345\t+ agreement selection\n346\t\n347\t71.69\n348\t\n349\t78.60\n350\t\n351\t75.15\n352\t\n353\t+ structured correction\n354\t\n355\t73.00\n356\t\n357\t79.60\n358\t\n359\t76.30\n360\t\n361\tAgreement selection chose the point-guided track for 91 of the 100 valid_u\n362\tqueries and the frame-level track for nine. The rule nevertheless improves\n363\tthe mean because it changes only complete tracks whose masks are better\n364\tsupported by the other candidates. This conservative behavior also explains\n365\twhy the main point-guided track remains dominant on the test set.\n366\t\n367\t4.4 Presence Classification\n368\t\n369\tTable 2 reports video-grouped OOF accuracy. The classifier\n370\twas first designed on the training split and then refit to valid_u\n371\twithout changing its inputs, feature transformations, regularization, or\n372\ttarget-recall constraint. The lower valid_u N-acc. indicates a\n373\tshift in absent-target expressions, while target recall remains above the\n374\tpredefined 0.97 requirement.\n375\t\n376\tTable 2: Video-grouped out-of-fold target-presence results. The model design\n377\tis fixed before the valid_u refit.\n378\t\n379\tSplit\n380\t\n381\tQueries\n382\t\n383\tVideos\n384\t\n385\tN-acc.\n386\t\n387\tT-acc.\n388\t\n389\tTraining OOF\n390\t\n391\t781\n392\t\n393\t75\n394\t\n395\t94.94\n396\t\n397\t97.68\n398\t\n399\tvalid_u OOF\n400\t\n401\t907\n402\t\n403\t50\n404\t\n405\t81.58\n406\t\n407\t97.58\n408\t\n409\t4.5 Official Test Result\n410\t\n411\tTable 3 gives the score returned by the official server for the\n412\tsubmitted package (submission ID 865991). The organizers subsequently\n413\tnotified our team that the entry placed first in the MeViS-Audio track. The\n414\tresult also shows that existence prediction is a major part of this benchmark:\n415\tN-acc. and T-acc. contribute two thirds of the final score.\n416\t\n417\tTable 3: Official hidden-test result of our final submission.\n418\t\n419\tMethod\n420\t\n421\t𝒥\\mathcal{J}\n422\t\n423\tℱ\\mathcal{F}\n424\t\n425\t𝒥&ℱ\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}\n426\t\n427\tN-acc.\n428\t\n429\tT-acc.\n430\t\n431\tFinal\n432\t\n433\tOurs\n434\t\n435\t0.5698\n436\t\n437\t0.6205\n438\t\n439\t0.5952\n440\t\n441\t0.7931\n442\t\n443\t0.9205\n444\t\n445\t0.769589\n446\t\n447\t5 Discussion and Limitations\n448\t\n449\tThe experiments support two practical observations. First, a strong spatial\n450\tpointing model followed by a video mask propagator is an effective main path,\n451\tbut independent tracks still help identify occasional identity switches and\n452\tmissed objects. Comparing masks is more stable than comparing model-specific\n453\tconfidence scores. Second, presence classification should be handled at the\n454\tvideo-query level. Within-video relative features are useful because many\n455\tqueries refer to related objects in the same scene and raw multimodal scores\n456\tare not directly comparable across videos.\n457\t\n458\tThe method also has clear limitations. Agreement can preserve a common error\n459\twhen all candidates follow the same distractor. The structured rules cover\n460\tonly explicit direction, count, and plural expressions and do not perform\n461\tgeneral language reasoning. Small objects and long occlusions remain hard for\n462\tpoint-guided propagation. Finally, the drop from valid_u mask quality to\n463\tthe hidden-test 𝒥&ℱ\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F} shows that the two domains differ substantially.\n464\tFuture work should replace hand-written structured cases with a trained\n465\ttemporal grounding model and calibrate target presence on a larger variety of\n466\tno-target examples.\n467\t\n468\t6 Conclusion\n469\t\n470\tWe presented the winning entry of the ECCV 2026 8th LSVOS MeViS-Audio track.\n471\tThe system separates audio transcription, video mask generation, track\n472\tselection, structured correction, and target-presence classification. Its\n473\tcentral mask rule selects the candidate that agrees most with the other full\n474\ttracks and requires no test labels. This simple modular design achieved a\n475\tfinal official score of 0.769589 and can be reproduced from fixed model\n476\tcheckpoints and deterministic post-processing rules.\n477\t\n478\tReferences\n479\t\n480\t[1]\n481\t\n482\tBai, S., Cai, Y., Chen, R., Chen, K., Chen, X., Cheng, Z., Deng, L., Ding, W., Gao, C., Ge, C., et al.: Qwen3-VL technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2511.21631 (2025)\n483\t\n484\t[2]\n485\t\n486\tCarion, N., Gustafson, L., Hu, Y.T., Debnath, S., Hu, R., Suris, D., Ryali, C., Alwala, K.V., Khedr, H., Huang, A., et al.: SAM 3: Segment anything with concepts. arXiv preprint arXiv:2511.16719 (2025)\n487\t\n488\t[3]\n489\t\n490\tClark, C., Yang, Y., Park, J.S., Ma, Z., Zhang, J., Tripathi, R., Salehi, M., Lee, S., Anderson, T., Han, W., Krishna, R.: MolmoPoint: Better pointing for VLMs with grounding tokens. arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.28069 (2026)\n491\t\n492\t[4]\n493\t\n494\tDing, H., Liu, C., He, S., Jiang, X., Loy, C.C.: MeViS: A large-scale benchmark for video segmentation with motion expressions. In: Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision. pp. 2694–2703 (2023)\n495\t\n496\t[5]\n497\t\n498\tDing, H., Liu, C., He, S., Ying, K., Jiang, X., Loy, C.C., Jiang, Y.G.: MeViS: A multi-modal dataset for referring motion expression video segmentation. IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence 47(12), 11400–11416 (2025). https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2025.3600507\n499\t\n500\t[6]\n501\t\n502\tDing, H., Ying, K., Liu, C., He, S., Jiang, X., Jiang, Y.G., Torr, P.H.S., Bai, S.: MOSEv2: A more challenging dataset for video object segmentation in complex scenes. arXiv preprint arXiv:2508.05630 (2025)\n503\t\n504\t[7]\n505\t\n506\tHong, J., Do, J.: 3rd place of MeViS-Audio track of the 5th PVUW: VIRST-Audio. arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.23126 (2026)\n507\t\n508\t[8]\n509\t\n510\tMiao, D., Gu, Y., Yang, C., Li, X., Zhang, H., Yang, M.H.: APRVOS: 1st place winner of 5th PVUW MeViS-Audio track. arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.18665 (2026)\n511\t\n512\t[9]\n513\t\n514\tNiu, Q., Gong, D., Chen, S., Zhang, T., Zhou, Y., Yuan, H., Qi, L., Li, X., Ji, S.: The 1st solution for 7th LSVOS RVOS track: SaSaSa2VA. arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.16972 (2025)\n515\t\n516\t[10]\n517\t\n518\tPont-Tuset, J., Perazzi, F., Caelles, S., Arbeláez, P., Sorkine-Hornung, A., Van Gool, L.: The 2017 DAVIS challenge on video object segmentation. arXiv preprint arXiv:1704.00675 (2017)\n519\t\n520\t[11]\n521\t\n522\tRavi, N., Gabeur, V., Hu, Y.T., Hu, R., Ryali, C., Ma, T., Khedr, H., Rädle, R., Rolland, C., Gustafson, L., et al.: SAM 2: Segment anything in images and videos. arXiv preprint arXiv:2408.00714 (2024)\n523\t\n524\t[12]\n525\t\n526\tShi, X., Wang, X., Guo, Z., Wang, Y., Zhang, P., Zhang, X., Guo, Z., Hao, H., Xi, Y., Yang, B., et al.: Qwen3-ASR technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.21337 (2026)\n527\t\n528\t[13]\n529\t\n530\tWang, Z., Kang, X., Li, S.: 2nd of the 5th PVUW MeViS-Audio track: ASR-SaSaSa2VA. arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.23935 (2026)\n531\t\n532\t[14]\n533\t\n534\tXu, J., Guo, Z., He, J., Hu, H., He, T., Bai, S., Chen, K., Wang, J., Fan, Y., Dang, K., et al.: Qwen2.5-Omni technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.20215 (2025)\n535\t\n536\t Experimental support, please\n537\t view the build logs\n538\t for errors. Generated by\n539\t\n540\t L\n541\t A\n542\t T\n543\t E\n544\t\n545\t xml\n546\t\n547\t .\n548\t\n549\tInstructions for reporting errors\n550\t\n551\tWe are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile\n552\t support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the\n553\t methods listed below:\n554\t\n555\tClick the \"Report Issue\" (\n556\t\n557\t ) button, located in the page header.\n558\t\n559\tTip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report.\n560\t\n561\tOur team has already identified the following issues. We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we\n562\t may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability\n563\t should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for\n564\t all.\n565\t\n566\tHave a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a list of packages that need conversion, and welcome developer contributions.\n567\t\n568\t We gratefully acknowledge support from\n569\t our major funders,\n570\t member institutions, ,\n571\t and all contributors.\n572\t\n573\t About\n574\t ·\n575\t Help\n576\t ·\n577\t Contact\n578\t ·\n579\t Subscribe\n580\t ·\n581\t Copyright\n582\t ·\n583\t Privacy\n584\t ·\n585\t Accessibility\n586\t ·\n587\t Operational Status (opens in new tab)\n588\t\n589\tMajor funding support from\n590\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "c74d23a3-81d8-4027-a799-59fbeebda3c4", "uuid": "566d8a65-18f3-4759-9c8e-0a9c58073cfb", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T12:32:53.012Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_full.txt", "content": "Agreement-Based Audio-Visual Segmentation: Champion Report for the MeViS-Audio Track in the 8th LSVOS Challenge\n\nReport GitHub Issue\n\n ×\n\n Title:\n\nContent selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n\n Description:\n\n Submit without GitHub\n Submit in GitHub\n\n arXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n Learn more\n ×\n\n Back to arXiv\n\n Why HTML?\n\n Report Issue\n\n Back to Abstract\n\n Download PDF\n\nAbstract\n\n1 Introduction\n\n2 Challenge and Dataset\n\n3 Method\n\n3.1 Overview\n\n3.2 Audio Transcription\n\n3.3 Candidate Mask Tracks\n\nPoint-guided SAM3 track.\n\nComplementary tracks.\n\n3.4 Agreement-Based Track Selection\n\n3.5 Structured Correction\n\n3.6 Target-Presence Classification\n\n4 Experiments\n\n4.1 Evaluation Protocol\n\n4.2 Implementation Details\n\n4.3 Mask Components\n\n4.4 Presence Classification\n\n4.5 Official Test Result\n\n5 Discussion and Limitations\n\n6 Conclusion\n\nReferences\n\n License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n\narXiv:2608.09475v1 [cs.CV] 10 Aug 2026\n\n11institutetext: National 111 Project Base of Intelligent Information Processing\n\nAgreement-Based Audio-Visual Segmentation: Champion Report for the MeViS-Audio Track in the 8th LSVOS Challenge\n\nYiwen Ren\n\nJianing Liu\n\nYingxin Wang\n\nKexin Zhang\n\nLicheng Jiao\n\nLingling Li\n\nXu Liu\n\nAbstract\n\nThe MeViS-Audio track asks a system to segment the objects described by a\nspoken motion expression throughout a video and to return empty masks when the\ndescribed target is absent. We present a simple staged solution. Qwen3-ASR\nfirst converts speech into text. Several video mask tracks are then produced\nwith complementary grounding and segmentation models. Instead of trusting a\nsingle prediction, we select the track that has the highest average mask\nagreement with the other candidates. A small set of explicit direction,\ncount, and plural rules corrects queries that require more than ordinary\nsingle-object tracking. Finally, a video-level classifier combines visual,\naudio-visual, and within-video query scores to decide whether any target is\npresent. The submitted system obtains 0.5952 𝒥&ℱ\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}, 0.7931 no-target\naccuracy, 0.9205 target accuracy, and a final score of 0.769589. The challenge\norganizers notified our team that this result ranked first in the track.\n\n1 Introduction\n\nReferring video object segmentation predicts a binary mask sequence for the\nobject described by a language query. MeViS emphasizes motion expressions,\nwhich often cannot be resolved from a single frame [4, 5]. The Audio track adds another source of uncertainty: the query\nis spoken and must first be understood from audio. Recognition errors can\nchange the object, its motion, or its number. Moreover, some queries have no\nmatching target in the video, so a segmenter must sometimes return an empty\nsequence rather than the most plausible visible object.\n\nEarlier MeViS-Audio systems established a useful modular design. APRVOS uses\nspeech recognition, visual existence checking, video segmentation, and mask\nrefinement [8]. ASR-SaSaSa2VA combines Qwen3-ASR,\nSaSaSa2VA, and a separate no-target classifier [13]. VIRST-Audio\nalso separates transcription, segmentation, and existence prediction\n [7]. These systems show that speech recognition,\npixel-level tracking, and target existence are different decisions and should\nnot be forced into one output score.\n\nOur system follows this modular view but reduces dependence on any single\nvideo segmenter. It creates several full-video mask tracks and selects the\none most consistent with the others. The selection uses only predicted masks,\nnot validation or test labels. Explicit motion and count rules are applied\nonly to queries for which the ordinary single-target assumption is\ninsufficient. A final presence classifier handles no-target inputs while\nprotecting high-confidence structured predictions.\n\nThe main contributions are:\n\n• \n\nan audio-to-mask pipeline that combines strong open models without\nend-to-end retraining;\n\n• \n\na label-free, agreement-based rule for selecting a complete video mask\ntrack, followed by a small structured correction stage\n\n• \n\na video-level target-presence classifier designed to maintain high\nrecall on valid targets while improving no-target accuracy.\n\n2 Challenge and Dataset\n\nThe 8th LSVOS Challenge at ECCV 2026 contains three tracks. The\nMOSEv2 track evaluates class-agnostic video object segmentation in\ncrowded scenes with occlusion, disappearance, small objects, and distractors;\nit uses the MOSEv2 dataset [6]. The\nMeViS-Text track evaluates referring video object segmentation from\nwritten motion expressions. The MeViS-Audio track uses spoken\nversions of motion expressions and additionally evaluates whether the referred\ntarget exists. Both referring tracks are based on MeViS v2\n [5].\n\nMeViS contains 2,006 videos, 8,171 annotated objects, and 33,072 motion\nexpressions in text and audio [5]. Unlike referring\nexpressions dominated by color or object category, many MeViS queries describe\nrelative motion, temporal order, or interactions. Correctly identifying the\ntarget therefore requires evidence across time. For the Audio track, the\ninput is a video V={It}t=1TV=\\{I_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T} and an audio query AA. The output is a\nmask sequence M={mt}t=1TM=\\{m_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{T}. If the query has no matching target, all\nmtm_{t} must be empty.\n\n3 Method\n\n3.1 Overview\n\nFigure 1 shows the complete inference path. The method first\ntranscribes the audio, then builds several candidate mask tracks. An\nagreement rule chooses one complete track per query. Structured corrections\nare applied to a small set of direction, count, and plural queries. Finally,\nthe presence classifier either keeps the masks or replaces the whole sequence\nwith empty masks.\n\nFigure 1: System overview. Audio is transcribed once, and all visual modules\nreceive the same text query. Candidate tracks are selected by mask agreement;\nthe final presence decision either keeps the masks or returns an empty sequence.\n\n3.2 Audio Transcription\n\nWe use Qwen3-ASR-1.7B [12] to convert the audio query into a\ntext expression\n\nq=ASR⁡(A).q=\\operatorname{ASR}(A).\n\n(1)\n\nKeeping transcription separate makes the visual stages easier to inspect and\nallows all candidate generators to receive exactly the same expression. The\nASR model produced transcripts for all 444 test queries.\n\n3.3 Candidate Mask Tracks\n\nPoint-guided SAM3 track.\n\nThe main candidate uses MolmoPoint-8B [3] to predict\nobject points with timestamps from the video and transcript. The video is\nencoded at 6 frames per second and the prompt requests points at 1 frame per\nsecond. We use at most 128 input frames, with deterministic 96- and 64-frame\nfallbacks when necessary. These points initialize SAM3\n [2], which propagates masks through the original video. A\nparallel SAM2.1 propagation [11] provides an independent\nconsistency signal for the frame-level fallback path.\n\nComplementary tracks.\n\nWe retain three candidates with different error patterns. SaSaSa2VA-26B\n [9] directly predicts a text-conditioned video mask track\nusing its wrap-around-plus inference mode. A frame-level planner chooses\nbetween point-guided and fallback masks using point hits, agreement between\nSAM2.1 and SAM3, support from neighboring frames, and mask similarity. A\nstructured track is generated for queries that explicitly state horizontal\ndirection, object count, or plural subjects. Qwen3-VL-30B-A3B\n [1] reads 16 sampled frames to extract these constraints,\nand SAM3 forms the corresponding candidate tracks.\n\nThe point-guided route is available for 407 of the 444 test queries. Queries\nwithout a complete set of candidates use the full-coverage frame-level track.\n\n3.4 Agreement-Based Track Selection\n\nFor a query with KK candidate tracks, let MitM_{i}^{t} be the mask from candidate\nii at frame tt. We first compute the mean temporal overlap between every\npair:\n\na​(i,j)=1T​∑t=1TIoU⁡(Mit,Mjt).a(i,j)=\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\operatorname{IoU}(M_{i}^{t},M_{j}^{t}).\n\n(2)\n\nWhen both masks are empty, their frame-level overlap is defined as one. The\nscore for candidate ii is its mean agreement with all other tracks,\n\ns​(i)=1K−1​∑j≠ia​(i,j),i∗=arg⁡maxi⁡s​(i).s(i)=\\frac{1}{K-1}\\sum_{j\\neq i}a(i,j),\\qquad i^{*}=\\arg\\max_{i}s(i).\n\n(3)\n\nThe output is the complete track Mi∗M_{i^{*}}. This is the medoid of the candidate\nset under mask overlap, but in the remainder of the paper we simply call it\nagreement-based selection. It needs no ground-truth labels and avoids\ncalibrating confidence values from unrelated models.\n\nOn the test set, the selected source was point-guided SAM3 for 403 queries and\nthe structured candidate for two. The frame-level and SaSaSa2VA candidates\nwere each selected once. These counts cover the 407 queries with a full\ncandidate set.\n\n3.5 Structured Correction\n\nOrdinary track selection can preserve a visually plausible answer while still\nmissing a constraint such as “moving left,” “two people,” or a plural\nsubject. We therefore apply a separate, conservative correction after\nagreement selection. A query is considered only if its transcript contains\nan explicit direction, number, or plural construction. Candidate tracks are\nthen checked for the requested horizontal motion and count; plural targets are\ncombined by mask union. This stage changed 25 of the 444 test queries. The\nrules and thresholds were fixed before test inference.\n\n3.6 Target-Presence Classification\n\nThe final stage predicts whether the transcript has a matching visual target.\nIt combines three scores: a Qwen3-VL-8B visual score, a direct Qwen2.5-Omni\naudio-visual score [14], and the relative rank of the query\namong all expressions for the same video. For each score we compute seven\nfeatures: its raw value, within-video percentile, standard and robust\nzz-scores, gaps to the video-level maximum and minimum, and the logarithm of\nthe number of queries. The resulting 21 features are passed to a balanced\nlogistic regression classifier.\n\nThe feature design and regularization were fixed on the training data. For\nthe development domain, the classifier weights and threshold were refit with\nvideo-grouped five-fold predictions. The threshold was selected under a\nminimum target recall of 0.97. If the predicted probability is below the\nthreshold, all masks are replaced by empty masks. As a safeguard, a query\naccepted by the structured correction is not removed solely by the presence\nclassifier. This safeguard restored three structured test predictions. The\nfinal classifier explicitly marked 42 test queries as no-target.\n\n4 Experiments\n\n4.1 Evaluation Protocol\n\nMask quality is measured with region overlap 𝒥\\mathcal{J} and boundary accuracy ℱ\\mathcal{F}\nfollowing DAVIS [10]; 𝒥&ℱ\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F} is their mean. Target\naccuracy (T-acc.) is the fraction of target-present queries kept by the\npresence classifier, while no-target accuracy (N-acc.) is the fraction of\ntarget-absent queries correctly rejected. The challenge score is\n\nFinal=𝒥&ℱ+N-acc.+T-acc.3.\\text{Final}=\\frac{\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}+\\text{N-acc.}+\\text{T-acc.}}{3}.\n\n(4)\n\nMask component studies use a frozen set of 100 target-present queries from the\nofficial valid_u split. Presence results are reported as out-of-fold\n(OOF) predictions with videos kept intact across folds. No test masks or\nper-query test labels were available when fixing the final rules.\n\n4.2 Implementation Details\n\nQwen3-ASR-1.7B is used without task-specific fine-tuning. MolmoPoint-8B runs\nin 4-bit form, and Qwen3-VL-30B-A3B uses its released AWQ weights. SAM3 and\nSAM2.1 propagate masks at the original frame resolution. The SaSaSa2VA\ncandidate is the 26B checkpoint. Agreement selection uses the same four\ncandidate types and the same\nrule on validation and test data. The final archive was checked against the\nsample submission and contains 28,415 PNG masks in 50 video directories.\n\n4.3 Mask Components\n\nTable 1 isolates the two decisions added after the main\npoint-guided SAM3 candidate. Agreement-based selection improves both region\nand boundary quality. Structured correction brings a further 1.15-point\ngain in 𝒥&ℱ\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}, with the largest change in queries involving direction or\nmultiple objects.\n\nTable 1: Mask component study on 100 target-present valid_u queries.\nAll values are percentages.\n\nMethod\n\n𝒥\\mathcal{J}\n\nℱ\\mathcal{F}\n\n𝒥&ℱ\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}\n\nPoint-guided SAM3\n\n71.07\n\n78.13\n\n74.60\n\n+ agreement selection\n\n71.69\n\n78.60\n\n75.15\n\n+ structured correction\n\n73.00\n\n79.60\n\n76.30\n\nAgreement selection chose the point-guided track for 91 of the 100 valid_u\nqueries and the frame-level track for nine. The rule nevertheless improves\nthe mean because it changes only complete tracks whose masks are better\nsupported by the other candidates. This conservative behavior also explains\nwhy the main point-guided track remains dominant on the test set.\n\n4.4 Presence Classification\n\nTable 2 reports video-grouped OOF accuracy. The classifier\nwas first designed on the training split and then refit to valid_u\nwithout changing its inputs, feature transformations, regularization, or\ntarget-recall constraint. The lower valid_u N-acc. indicates a\nshift in absent-target expressions, while target recall remains above the\npredefined 0.97 requirement.\n\nTable 2: Video-grouped out-of-fold target-presence results. The model design\nis fixed before the valid_u refit.\n\nSplit\n\nQueries\n\nVideos\n\nN-acc.\n\nT-acc.\n\nTraining OOF\n\n781\n\n75\n\n94.94\n\n97.68\n\nvalid_u OOF\n\n907\n\n50\n\n81.58\n\n97.58\n\n4.5 Official Test Result\n\nTable 3 gives the score returned by the official server for the\nsubmitted package (submission ID 865991). The organizers subsequently\nnotified our team that the entry placed first in the MeViS-Audio track. The\nresult also shows that existence prediction is a major part of this benchmark:\nN-acc. and T-acc. contribute two thirds of the final score.\n\nTable 3: Official hidden-test result of our final submission.\n\nMethod\n\n𝒥\\mathcal{J}\n\nℱ\\mathcal{F}\n\n𝒥&ℱ\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F}\n\nN-acc.\n\nT-acc.\n\nFinal\n\nOurs\n\n0.5698\n\n0.6205\n\n0.5952\n\n0.7931\n\n0.9205\n\n0.769589\n\n5 Discussion and Limitations\n\nThe experiments support two practical observations. First, a strong spatial\npointing model followed by a video mask propagator is an effective main path,\nbut independent tracks still help identify occasional identity switches and\nmissed objects. Comparing masks is more stable than comparing model-specific\nconfidence scores. Second, presence classification should be handled at the\nvideo-query level. Within-video relative features are useful because many\nqueries refer to related objects in the same scene and raw multimodal scores\nare not directly comparable across videos.\n\nThe method also has clear limitations. Agreement can preserve a common error\nwhen all candidates follow the same distractor. The structured rules cover\nonly explicit direction, count, and plural expressions and do not perform\ngeneral language reasoning. Small objects and long occlusions remain hard for\npoint-guided propagation. Finally, the drop from valid_u mask quality to\nthe hidden-test 𝒥&ℱ\\mathcal{J}\\&\\mathcal{F} shows that the two domains differ substantially.\nFuture work should replace hand-written structured cases with a trained\ntemporal grounding model and calibrate target presence on a larger variety of\nno-target examples.\n\n6 Conclusion\n\nWe presented the winning entry of the ECCV 2026 8th LSVOS MeViS-Audio track.\nThe system separates audio transcription, video mask generation, track\nselection, structured correction, and target-presence classification. Its\ncentral mask rule selects the candidate that agrees most with the other full\ntracks and requires no test labels. This simple modular design achieved a\nfinal official score of 0.769589 and can be reproduced from fixed model\ncheckpoints and deterministic post-processing rules.\n\nReferences\n\n[1]\n\nBai, S., Cai, Y., Chen, R., Chen, K., Chen, X., Cheng, Z., Deng, L., Ding, W., Gao, C., Ge, C., et al.: Qwen3-VL technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2511.21631 (2025)\n\n[2]\n\nCarion, N., Gustafson, L., Hu, Y.T., Debnath, S., Hu, R., Suris, D., Ryali, C., Alwala, K.V., Khedr, H., Huang, A., et al.: SAM 3: Segment anything with concepts. arXiv preprint arXiv:2511.16719 (2025)\n\n[3]\n\nClark, C., Yang, Y., Park, J.S., Ma, Z., Zhang, J., Tripathi, R., Salehi, M., Lee, S., Anderson, T., Han, W., Krishna, R.: MolmoPoint: Better pointing for VLMs with grounding tokens. arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.28069 (2026)\n\n[4]\n\nDing, H., Liu, C., He, S., Jiang, X., Loy, C.C.: MeViS: A large-scale benchmark for video segmentation with motion expressions. In: Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision. pp. 2694–2703 (2023)\n\n[5]\n\nDing, H., Liu, C., He, S., Ying, K., Jiang, X., Loy, C.C., Jiang, Y.G.: MeViS: A multi-modal dataset for referring motion expression video segmentation. IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence 47(12), 11400–11416 (2025). https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2025.3600507\n\n[6]\n\nDing, H., Ying, K., Liu, C., He, S., Jiang, X., Jiang, Y.G., Torr, P.H.S., Bai, S.: MOSEv2: A more challenging dataset for video object segmentation in complex scenes. arXiv preprint arXiv:2508.05630 (2025)\n\n[7]\n\nHong, J., Do, J.: 3rd place of MeViS-Audio track of the 5th PVUW: VIRST-Audio. arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.23126 (2026)\n\n[8]\n\nMiao, D., Gu, Y., Yang, C., Li, X., Zhang, H., Yang, M.H.: APRVOS: 1st place winner of 5th PVUW MeViS-Audio track. arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.18665 (2026)\n\n[9]\n\nNiu, Q., Gong, D., Chen, S., Zhang, T., Zhou, Y., Yuan, H., Qi, L., Li, X., Ji, S.: The 1st solution for 7th LSVOS RVOS track: SaSaSa2VA. arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.16972 (2025)\n\n[10]\n\nPont-Tuset, J., Perazzi, F., Caelles, S., Arbeláez, P., Sorkine-Hornung, A., Van Gool, L.: The 2017 DAVIS challenge on video object segmentation. arXiv preprint arXiv:1704.00675 (2017)\n\n[11]\n\nRavi, N., Gabeur, V., Hu, Y.T., Hu, R., Ryali, C., Ma, T., Khedr, H., Rädle, R., Rolland, C., Gustafson, L., et al.: SAM 2: Segment anything in images and videos. arXiv preprint arXiv:2408.00714 (2024)\n\n[12]\n\nShi, X., Wang, X., Guo, Z., Wang, Y., Zhang, P., Zhang, X., Guo, Z., Hao, H., Xi, Y., Yang, B., et al.: Qwen3-ASR technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.21337 (2026)\n\n[13]\n\nWang, Z., Kang, X., Li, S.: 2nd of the 5th PVUW MeViS-Audio track: ASR-SaSaSa2VA. arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.23935 (2026)\n\n[14]\n\nXu, J., Guo, Z., He, J., Hu, H., He, T., Bai, S., Chen, K., Wang, J., Fan, Y., Dang, K., et al.: Qwen2.5-Omni technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.20215 (2025)\n\n Experimental support, please\n view the build logs\n for errors. Generated by\n\n L\n A\n T\n E\n\n xml\n\n .\n\nInstructions for reporting errors\n\nWe are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile\n support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the\n methods listed below:\n\nClick the \"Report Issue\" (\n\n ) button, located in the page header.\n\nTip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report.\n\nOur team has already identified the following issues. We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we\n may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability\n should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for\n all.\n\nHave a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a list of packages that need conversion, and welcome developer contributions.\n\n We gratefully acknowledge support from\n our major funders,\n member institutions, ,\n and all contributors.\n\n About\n ·\n Help\n ·\n Contact\n ·\n Subscribe\n ·\n Copyright\n ·\n Privacy\n ·\n Accessibility\n ·\n Operational Status (opens in new tab)\n\nMajor funding support from\n", "numLines": 590, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 590}}}
